1
00:00:03,058 --> 00:00:03,358
to you.

2
00:00:13,872 --> 00:00:17,656
I didn't expect it to be this popular.

3
00:00:17,676 --> 00:00:18,877
Thank you for coming out.

4
00:00:19,397 --> 00:00:21,640
So let's first go over who I am.

5
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:22,781
My name is David Brevik, and I am a

6
00:00:35,332 --> 00:00:38,595
was the president of Blizzard North for about ten years.

7
00:00:39,456 --> 00:00:45,020
That's me there on the left with Max and Eric Schaefer, who were the other co-founders of Blizzard North.

8
00:00:45,981 --> 00:00:54,687
And I was lead programmer on Diablo as well as co-creator and co-designer and et cetera, as well as kind of the original concept creator.

9
00:00:54,807 --> 00:00:57,910
So the idea for Diablo was

10
00:00:59,972 --> 00:01:04,337
started, oh wait, before we go to that, let's talk a little bit about what we're here for.

11
00:01:04,357 --> 00:01:06,019
We're here to talk about Diablo.

12
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:09,163
It was released December 31st, 1996.

13
00:01:09,324 --> 00:01:12,067
That makes this the 20th anniversary year.

14
00:01:12,728 --> 00:01:14,170
It'll be 20th anniversary

15
00:01:14,710 --> 00:01:15,991
the very last day of this year.

16
00:01:16,391 --> 00:01:19,032
Some people say that it came out in the very beginning of January.

17
00:01:19,393 --> 00:01:32,620
Technically, if you were on the West Coast, you got some boxes at the stores on the 31st, but then it was probably January 2nd or 3rd before you actually got it in a lot of places in the U.S. because of the way that shipping worked and things like that at the time.

18
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:37,301
I'm here to do a post-mortem on a game.

19
00:01:37,781 --> 00:01:39,242
Now, it may be a little early.

20
00:01:39,982 --> 00:01:50,466
I don't know if we had enough time to digest whether or not the entire gravity of the situation making this game, but I hope to do it some justice.

21
00:01:51,166 --> 00:01:56,168
28 years ago is a long time ago, and my memory is not as sharp as it used to be.

22
00:01:57,248 --> 00:01:59,691
So let's go through this and we'll see how it goes.

23
00:02:00,592 --> 00:02:02,333
First, the original concept.

24
00:02:02,413 --> 00:02:06,137
The original concept was something I thought of in high school.

25
00:02:07,258 --> 00:02:18,189
I lived here my junior and senior year of high school out in the East Bay, east part of San Francisco Bay Area, in a town called Danville.

26
00:02:19,227 --> 00:02:26,696
and I lived at the base of Mount Diablo, and that is where the name comes from.

27
00:02:29,099 --> 00:02:33,464
I didn't speak a lick of Spanish when I moved here, and then I found out what the name meant.

28
00:02:33,865 --> 00:02:35,406
I was like, yeah, that name rocks.

29
00:02:36,368 --> 00:02:38,090
I'm going to use that someday.

30
00:02:39,882 --> 00:02:44,404
I was very fortunate to know from a very early age exactly what I wanted to do.

31
00:02:45,144 --> 00:02:58,270
My dad brought home an Apple II Plus when I was young, and I taught myself to program and played games and programmed nonstop pretty much all the way, well, even today.

32
00:02:58,370 --> 00:03:00,531
So I haven't stopped, really.

33
00:03:00,631 --> 00:03:02,752
And it's all I've ever wanted to do, make games.

34
00:03:02,832 --> 00:03:07,334
And so even in high school, I was thinking about what kind of games I could make and what kind of names I could use.

35
00:03:08,154 --> 00:03:11,315
and the concept was very different at that time.

36
00:03:11,335 --> 00:03:24,861
It was much more kind of a party-based wizardry kind of game, you know, might and magic and wizardry and that kind of style that it was, that it ended up being.

37
00:03:25,761 --> 00:03:31,243
It really was influenced heavily through my time in college by a lot of roguelikes, including

38
00:03:31,323 --> 00:03:43,950
including Rogue and NetHack and Moria and Angband and games like that and I played them on these Unix machines when I was at college before the internet existed.

39
00:03:45,571 --> 00:03:52,876
And so it existed but it was just like the universities were hooked up to the government services and stuff like that.

40
00:03:53,016 --> 00:03:56,578
It was not HTML and stuff had not really been invented at that time.

41
00:03:59,932 --> 00:04:09,000
Then I graduated school and we decided that I worked very briefly at a few different places, including being technical director at Iguana.

42
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:13,504
Iguana's claim to fame was really their NBA Jam games.

43
00:04:14,284 --> 00:04:16,846
And so they moved to Texas.

44
00:04:16,906 --> 00:04:18,328
They started out here in the Bay Area.

45
00:04:21,491 --> 00:04:32,795
I left when they moved to Texas and started my own company called Condor with Max and Eric, these artists that I had met at a clip art company that I originally worked at right out of school.

46
00:04:33,256 --> 00:04:37,057
It was a clip art and then video game company that went under after six months.

47
00:04:38,858 --> 00:04:47,982
Anyway, so the name of the company we came up with was Condor, and Condor comes from that time when we were at the clip art company

48
00:04:48,882 --> 00:04:50,644
And we knew it was kind of a mess.

49
00:04:51,604 --> 00:04:55,047
And so we were all kind of dreaming about what day we'll make our own games.

50
00:04:55,107 --> 00:04:59,751
And so the secret project was going to be Project Condor was the code name.

51
00:04:59,771 --> 00:05:02,434
Hey, we want to talk about Condor over lunch.

52
00:05:03,134 --> 00:05:07,498
And so we would go and chat and dream of ideas and things like that.

53
00:05:07,558 --> 00:05:11,361
But I kept coming back to this Diablo idea that I had.

54
00:05:13,343 --> 00:05:20,444
Anyway, so eventually we put it to paper and here is the original design document for Diablo.

55
00:05:21,285 --> 00:05:25,185
It was very similar to the way it turned out.

56
00:05:26,105 --> 00:05:29,806
Okay, no, it wasn't similar at all.

57
00:05:30,046 --> 00:05:32,007
First off, it was turn-based.

58
00:05:34,887 --> 00:05:36,787
Next, it was single-player.

59
00:05:38,968 --> 00:05:39,768
It was DOS.

60
00:05:42,434 --> 00:06:11,330
it had expansion packs like magic the gathering cards like you could buy a disc with like fifty items on it and install it and we could sell just tons of these packs that you could just add on and we quickly realized that that was a big issue uh... there was permadeath it was unforgiving i mean hardcore eventually made its way into the series thankfully but the uh... but uh... at the time it was uh... you know that was very staple

61
00:06:12,110 --> 00:06:15,351
feature of roguelikes, and so that's why I wanted that.

62
00:06:16,171 --> 00:06:24,074
And then there's a rumor, sort of, it's not really just a rumor, but that it had claymation.

63
00:06:25,755 --> 00:06:27,976
And that is kind of true.

64
00:06:30,069 --> 00:06:51,838
The original idea about the art style was that we were going to, and this was kind of about five years ago, Blizzard made a retrospective of Diablo and all their games or whatever, and in it they made this cute little funny jokey cartoon of this demon clay thing coming out and being attacked and falling over.

65
00:06:53,219 --> 00:07:06,693
There was a game at the time, it wasn't just Claymation, it was a game at the time that was called Primal Rage.

66
00:07:07,234 --> 00:07:12,379
I loved the way that this game looked in the arcade, I thought it was really neat.

67
00:07:13,380 --> 00:07:13,820
very much.

68
00:07:30,453 --> 00:07:32,895
Or if they did, you needed like weeks to render it.

69
00:07:33,415 --> 00:07:38,478
So we said, well, we'll give this a try.

70
00:07:38,498 --> 00:07:39,459
We'll see what this is like.

71
00:07:39,499 --> 00:07:41,560
And so we sat down and we looked into it.

72
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:43,441
And we're like, oh my god, we're not doing this.

73
00:07:43,461 --> 00:07:44,442
This is a disaster.

74
00:07:44,482 --> 00:07:49,605
There's no way we can make all these models and photograph all these things and put them in the game.

75
00:07:50,125 --> 00:07:53,747
So it was claymation for, and it wasn't really claymation.

76
00:07:53,767 --> 00:07:55,989
It was stop motion for about five seconds.

77
00:07:56,069 --> 00:07:57,350
So that was definitely.

78
00:07:58,911 --> 00:08:01,352
that puts that to rest.

79
00:08:02,273 --> 00:08:04,455
They say claymation, I say stop motion, but there you go.

80
00:08:05,636 --> 00:08:17,825
So the company Condor got contracts to do a bunch of different games, doing just contract work with publishers that we knew, Acclaim and Sunsoft and some others.

81
00:08:18,505 --> 00:08:23,489
And so we got a contract to do the Justice League Task Force game.

82
00:08:24,110 --> 00:08:26,832
And this was a Superman, Batman, Wonder Woman,

83
00:08:27,552 --> 00:08:35,979
Street Fighter clone, and so we were working on this and we were working on some football games.

84
00:08:36,780 --> 00:08:46,308
I'm a big sports fan, and so we were doing some NFL quarterback club Game Boy and Game Gear games at the time as well.

85
00:08:48,590 --> 00:08:51,232
So we show up at CES.

86
00:08:51,412 --> 00:08:53,394
This is before E3 existed.

87
00:08:54,474 --> 00:08:58,998
We used to have our ‑‑ I feel like such an old guy at this point.

88
00:08:59,778 --> 00:09:08,405
Back in my day, when we ‑‑ we had our show next to the car stereos, which were blowing us out the whole time.

89
00:09:09,505 --> 00:09:12,388
And so we show up, and

90
00:09:13,979 --> 00:09:23,046
where we have our game on display and we look over and wait there's another version of the product for Super Nintendo.

91
00:09:23,906 --> 00:09:25,888
We're like wait what?

92
00:09:26,868 --> 00:09:31,752
We didn't know that there was another developer working on the exact same game as us.

93
00:09:32,733 --> 00:09:34,014
The two had never talked.

94
00:09:34,214 --> 00:09:36,976
The publisher never talked about there was another contract.

95
00:09:36,996 --> 00:09:39,678
Like, what the heck is going on?

96
00:09:39,698 --> 00:09:41,039
This is really weird or whatever.

97
00:09:41,640 --> 00:09:44,002
And the games were strangely similar.

98
00:09:46,828 --> 00:09:50,009
I'm like, whoa, this is really weird.

99
00:09:50,469 --> 00:09:54,731
So we got to know these guys, and their name was Silicon and Synapse.

100
00:09:55,391 --> 00:10:03,774
And so we got to talking at the show, and they said, oh, yeah, well, we're Silicon and Synapse right now.

101
00:10:06,865 --> 00:10:08,266
kind of under the radar here.

102
00:10:09,466 --> 00:10:13,768
We're selling the company and we're going to change our name and we're going to make a PC game.

103
00:10:13,928 --> 00:10:15,828
And I said, oh, that sounds awesome.

104
00:10:15,848 --> 00:10:16,649
That's what we want to do.

105
00:10:16,669 --> 00:10:17,109
My dream.

106
00:10:17,149 --> 00:10:18,869
I have a dream to make a PC game.

107
00:10:20,050 --> 00:10:21,010
I love PC gaming.

108
00:10:21,050 --> 00:10:22,451
It's the main thing that I play.

109
00:10:22,491 --> 00:10:26,152
And someday I'm going to make this game.

110
00:10:26,732 --> 00:10:27,873
And we've been trying to

111
00:10:29,225 --> 00:10:44,931
pitch this game idea to a whole bunch of people, and we've gone around to 25 different publishers, this CES and previous ones, and handed out this document to a whole bunch of people, and they have said, no, RPGs are dead.

112
00:10:45,251 --> 00:10:48,153
There is no way we are investing in an RPG.

113
00:10:49,093 --> 00:10:49,993
It was ridiculous.

114
00:10:50,013 --> 00:10:51,374
You guys are out of your mind.

115
00:10:51,734 --> 00:10:59,398
And you guys not only want us to invest in an RPG, but you also want us to sell a crap ton of discs, and that's a logistic nightmare, and that's just never gonna happen.

116
00:11:00,698 --> 00:11:03,439
I said, but damn it, the game idea is solid, okay?

117
00:11:03,519 --> 00:11:06,081
It's kind of a modern version of Rogue or whatever.

118
00:11:07,396 --> 00:11:15,501
Anyway, so we got to talking to Silicon and Synapse, and they're like, oh, we're making this game or whatever, and we're going to change our name, and we're going to be called Blizzard Entertainment.

119
00:11:15,721 --> 00:11:17,942
And the game that we're making is called Warcraft.

120
00:11:18,983 --> 00:11:21,184
And I said, ooh, well, let me go look at it.

121
00:11:21,244 --> 00:11:27,328
So we went back, and they had this one room that was maybe 10 by 10, let's say.

122
00:11:27,368 --> 00:11:28,188
We'll be generous.

123
00:11:28,208 --> 00:11:34,492
A little 10 by 10 kind of walled in, you know, semi-permanent structure kind of meeting room.

124
00:11:35,312 --> 00:11:37,934
and back in the corner they're kind of geeking out and playing the game.

125
00:11:38,475 --> 00:11:42,198
And they have a couple posters and a couple press people in there and talking and stuff.

126
00:11:42,218 --> 00:11:43,419
And I thought it looked really great.

127
00:11:43,439 --> 00:11:45,380
I thought that it was amazing.

128
00:11:45,460 --> 00:11:49,123
And so a bunch of people heard the buzz at the show about this thing.

129
00:11:49,143 --> 00:11:54,507
And then as time went on, I kept kind of bugging them and saying, hey, you guys need some beta testers?

130
00:11:54,527 --> 00:11:54,847
Because

131
00:11:55,688 --> 00:11:59,812
I got a whole company full of beta testers if you guys need some beta testing.

132
00:12:00,753 --> 00:12:04,096
And eventually we got to talking more and more, and I said, all right, come on.

133
00:12:04,176 --> 00:12:06,738
After you guys, I've got this great game idea.

134
00:12:07,018 --> 00:12:13,064
They said, OK, well, after we're done with Warcraft 1, we'll come out and we'll listen to your pitch.

135
00:12:13,624 --> 00:12:15,386
I said, OK, well, that's all I can ask for.

136
00:12:15,426 --> 00:12:15,786
Thank you.

137
00:12:15,986 --> 00:12:19,429
And so they came out in January of 1995.

138
00:12:22,773 --> 00:12:25,774
correct, and doing math quickly in my head.

139
00:12:26,074 --> 00:12:26,254
No.

140
00:12:26,974 --> 00:12:27,235
Yes.

141
00:12:28,075 --> 00:12:28,335
Okay.

142
00:12:28,755 --> 00:12:29,595
Somewhere right in there.

143
00:12:32,076 --> 00:12:38,038
And so they came out, and we pitched them Diablo, and then kind of the rest is history.

144
00:12:38,859 --> 00:12:41,980
And so we signed the contract to do Diablo with them.

145
00:12:42,860 --> 00:12:45,281
We were very excited, because it's kind of this dream game.

146
00:12:45,301 --> 00:12:47,782
Now, at the same time, we also, we were kind of

147
00:12:48,342 --> 00:12:56,188
beginning development on Diablo, but we had to finish the development of Justice League and the Game Boy football games and things like that.

148
00:12:56,228 --> 00:13:07,376
So we had to kind of wrap this stuff up through the most of kind of the 95 year, about the first half of it, as well as start, you know, we had to sign these contracts and do all sorts of stuff to get rolling.

149
00:13:07,396 --> 00:13:09,198
And that kind of takes time and whatnot.

150
00:13:09,298 --> 00:13:13,961
And we started to hire some people and whatnot to try and work on this development.

151
00:13:13,981 --> 00:13:15,622
But one of the very first things that we did

152
00:13:16,663 --> 00:13:20,145
as we decided what is the game going to look like, right?

153
00:13:20,185 --> 00:13:22,646
What is kind of the graphic look of the game?

154
00:13:22,666 --> 00:13:28,689
One of the games at the time that we had been playing a lot and really loved was XCOM, and I still love that game to this day.

155
00:13:28,749 --> 00:13:36,232
I mean, there were a bunch of games actually during that era that SimTech made a whole bunch of really fantastic games right then.

156
00:13:36,753 --> 00:13:39,294
And so we took

157
00:13:40,833 --> 00:13:42,515
And this was not easy back then, by the way.

158
00:13:43,216 --> 00:13:47,802
I kind of took a screen grab of XCOM.

159
00:13:49,204 --> 00:13:50,685
It had a little cursor box.

160
00:13:52,140 --> 00:13:55,362
that showed exactly the tile size and et cetera.

161
00:13:55,382 --> 00:13:57,423
And we wanted to emulate this look.

162
00:13:58,084 --> 00:14:01,906
And so we just took that and we put it right into Diablo.

163
00:14:03,007 --> 00:14:09,371
And so the actual tile square basis is exactly the same in XCOM and Diablo.

164
00:14:09,891 --> 00:14:13,614
They are the exact same shape and size.

165
00:14:14,334 --> 00:14:21,139
So all of the kind of technology we built was upon basically, you know, a screenshot, a screen grab from XCOM.

166
00:14:25,323 --> 00:14:28,066
that's a really early version of the game there on the right.

167
00:14:28,106 --> 00:14:31,749
You can see that God, I don't even know what's going on in that picture.

168
00:14:31,769 --> 00:14:34,933
I don't even know what those big black boxes are.

169
00:14:34,973 --> 00:14:37,135
I have no idea what we were even thinking there.

170
00:14:37,575 --> 00:14:39,618
Anyway, very strange.

171
00:14:41,446 --> 00:14:41,866
who knows.

172
00:14:43,047 --> 00:14:46,248
It's got health and mana and health and mana are reversed.

173
00:14:46,288 --> 00:14:49,109
I don't even know what's going on in that thing.

174
00:14:49,530 --> 00:14:52,771
So I'm going to get a little bit technical here.

175
00:14:52,811 --> 00:14:56,573
I hope that I don't, you know, people start nodding off or something.

176
00:14:56,653 --> 00:14:57,633
I apologize.

177
00:14:57,653 --> 00:15:00,174
But I'm going to get slightly technical here.

178
00:15:01,315 --> 00:15:02,936
So one of the things that went right.

179
00:15:04,016 --> 00:15:06,157
Also, before we get into this, just a quick note.

180
00:15:07,178 --> 00:15:17,364
I went through and I looked at a bunch of, you know, post-mortem in my mind, it's like, hey, here's three to five things that we did right, here's three to five things we did wrong, boom, ba-da-boom, ba-da-boom, done, you know, and that's post-mortem.

181
00:15:17,804 --> 00:15:22,587
But I looked online and started watching some of the classic post-mortems and, like, they weren't doing that.

182
00:15:22,687 --> 00:15:26,330
And I was like, wait, okay, well,

183
00:15:27,190 --> 00:15:31,392
I already wrote my talk and it was all the 3 to 5 things that were good and 3 to 5 things that were bad.

184
00:15:31,412 --> 00:15:32,813
I don't know what I'm going to do.

185
00:15:32,833 --> 00:15:34,194
Crap, I've got to rewrite this thing.

186
00:15:34,214 --> 00:15:36,755
So I'm kind of merging my old talk with the new one.

187
00:15:36,815 --> 00:15:39,096
One of the things that went right here is lighting.

188
00:15:43,420 --> 00:15:58,434
we came up with this idea, actually I think Eric came up with the idea of doing lighting and arranging the palette in such a way that we have 256 colors at the time, this is super VGA, 640x480 graphics, just state of the art kind of stuff.

189
00:15:59,194 --> 00:16:01,897
And Eric came up with this idea of doing lighting and arranging

190
00:16:02,117 --> 00:16:06,701
And so we had, I mean 256 colors was a big deal.

191
00:16:07,061 --> 00:16:11,184
VGA 320 by 200, is that what it was?

192
00:16:11,825 --> 00:16:13,166
320 by 240?

193
00:16:13,226 --> 00:16:14,647
I don't remember.

194
00:16:15,248 --> 00:16:19,971
And that was kind of, 640, 480 was definitely a big leap up.

195
00:16:20,051 --> 00:16:20,992
And so we had these 256 colors.

196
00:16:22,373 --> 00:16:26,194
to work with, and so we kind of split the palette into two different sections.

197
00:16:26,214 --> 00:16:32,735
We split it into 128 colors for the background and 128 colors for everything else.

198
00:16:33,095 --> 00:16:45,137
That was the monsters, the UI, the characters, the spell effects, everything fit into one 256-color palette, and then the background into a, I mean, 128-color palette, and the other was, the background was 128 colors.

199
00:16:48,515 --> 00:16:50,056
I'm not really sure why we did it that way.

200
00:16:50,076 --> 00:16:56,438
We probably should have balanced it a little bit better and made like 64 for the background and the rest for everything else, but who knows?

201
00:16:56,478 --> 00:16:56,779
I don't know.

202
00:16:56,999 --> 00:16:58,579
We were dumb back then.

203
00:17:01,369 --> 00:17:14,897
Actually, funny story and a true story, Diablo 1 was actually the first time I ever coded in C. I had never done a C program in my life until I coded Diablo 1.

204
00:17:14,937 --> 00:17:20,080
And in fact, Diablo 1, a lot of the graphic routines and stuff are all written in assembly.

205
00:17:21,681 --> 00:17:26,404
So I'd say maybe like whatever, an eighth of all the code in Diablo was in assembly.

206
00:17:28,507 --> 00:17:34,649
So we used this and kind of organized the palette so that the colors went from dark to light.

207
00:17:35,089 --> 00:17:49,275
And so every strip of 16 colors, we had eight different hues for background, eight different hues and saturations, whatever you want to call them, and lightness levels going across so that you could go from dark to light.

208
00:17:49,635 --> 00:17:55,177
And so when we went to go draw the graph, OK, let's go through the art pipeline first.

209
00:17:55,677 --> 00:17:58,158
We've got, this is, I think this is

210
00:17:58,158 --> 00:17:58,198
is

211
00:18:18,582 --> 00:18:23,464
Here's the solid render of it, and here it is textured.

212
00:18:23,644 --> 00:18:27,466
Every article on video game making had this included.

213
00:18:27,486 --> 00:18:31,608
This was a standard issue art piece in every single article.

214
00:18:31,688 --> 00:18:38,031
Anyway, we would take this and we would actually render out the sprites, develop the models in 3D, just like it was actually this process.

215
00:18:38,251 --> 00:18:40,392
I don't think we ever went to a solid render.

216
00:18:42,553 --> 00:18:47,614
we would then export it, and it would render out a bunch of series of sprites on a one sheet.

217
00:18:47,895 --> 00:18:49,635
And so it was kind of some sprite sheet thing.

218
00:18:49,995 --> 00:19:00,618
Then we'd use this program called DeBabelizer that would map that palette, these 24-bit images, to this specific palette that we had.

219
00:19:00,678 --> 00:19:07,080
Whatever it was, if it was a background piece, into the background ones, and if otherwise, it would go into these,

220
00:19:08,440 --> 00:19:08,480
at

221
00:19:27,353 --> 00:19:27,453
and

222
00:19:44,110 --> 00:19:58,755
get the final light color, kind of the matching of the light color, and then, you know, I knew what hue it was, and that was the upper nibble, and the new bite, new nibble on the bottom became the actual color to use, and so then I could write that, and that is how we did the lighting.

223
00:19:58,795 --> 00:20:09,318
We had these kind of two different tables and set up the palette in that way, and it was something that really had not been seen before at the time, and it really led to the atmosphere of Diablo.

224
00:20:09,338 --> 00:20:13,039
It made it, you know, dark and creepy, and that was one of the

225
00:20:13,599 --> 00:20:16,622
one of the techniques that we use to really kind of bring out the atmosphere.

226
00:20:18,343 --> 00:20:21,045
So what went wrong in the graphics?

227
00:20:21,766 --> 00:20:30,253
And this one still gives me I just don't even I don't even know why we didn't change this even when we knew that it was a big problem.

228
00:20:31,578 --> 00:20:37,421
So what went wrong were the floors, or I guess the walls, or I guess both.

229
00:20:37,521 --> 00:20:43,363
So looking at this picture, you can see that how we drew the scene.

230
00:20:43,963 --> 00:20:51,507
And so the way that we would draw the scene is we would start it there at tile number one, you can imagine, and then tile two, and then three, and then four, and then five, and then six.

231
00:20:52,887 --> 00:21:05,131
Went from the back what was away from you and kind of moved forward left to right and in drew these tiles So when you drew the tile I drew the tile and then I drew all the stuff that was on top of the tile Right there.

232
00:21:05,271 --> 00:21:11,273
And so it's like oh, it's got a player there and it's got an item and you know It has a chest or whatever.

233
00:21:11,333 --> 00:21:17,535
And so it would it would draw whatever is on top of the tile so That worked really well

234
00:21:18,926 --> 00:21:22,514
until we actually attach the walls to the floor.

235
00:21:24,005 --> 00:21:31,747
And so when I went to go draw something and you were moving from one square to the other, your sprite would get cut off.

236
00:21:31,907 --> 00:21:38,168
You would be like halfway through your walk as you moved along, because you're here in this location in the one position.

237
00:21:38,488 --> 00:21:43,149
And as you moved to two, it would draw two after it would draw one.

238
00:21:43,209 --> 00:21:46,130
And so your sprite would just end up getting cut off.

239
00:21:46,390 --> 00:21:53,712
And so we had to fix this by actually moving you to the place that you're going to be downstream.

240
00:21:54,512 --> 00:22:00,918
and then kind of negatively offsetting you back to where you were and then slowly drawing you the other way.

241
00:22:00,998 --> 00:22:11,867
So in Diablo, and we had to program all sorts of AI stuff where it was like targeting the right place of where you were or going to be and all this kind of stuff.

242
00:22:12,347 --> 00:22:15,450
All we had to do was just separate the damn floor from the walls.

243
00:22:15,510 --> 00:22:17,471
I don't know why we didn't go back and do that.

244
00:22:18,452 --> 00:22:25,353
If we had just drawn all the floor and then drawn the walls and all the things on top of it, it would have solved a lot of these problems.

245
00:22:26,174 --> 00:22:28,334
Anyway, lessons learned.

246
00:22:28,374 --> 00:22:29,674
You don't have to worry about this anymore.

247
00:22:29,694 --> 00:22:31,415
The card does all this crap for you.

248
00:22:31,435 --> 00:22:31,835
Kids today.

249
00:22:31,875 --> 00:22:34,975
The big controversy.

250
00:22:34,995 --> 00:22:35,115
So,

251
00:22:50,547 --> 00:22:55,050
real time versus turn based.

