And then they said, okay, well, what went wrong?

423
00:37:53,241 --> 00:38:01,252
multiplayer we're gonna come up and we're gonna take a look at your multiplayer code and they came up and they're like you don't have any multiplayer code

424
00:38:04,041 --> 00:38:04,481
Oh, shit.

425
00:38:05,101 --> 00:38:05,481
All right.

426
00:38:05,541 --> 00:38:09,622
Well, in order for this to work with Battle.net, we're going to have to have multiplayer code.

427
00:38:09,682 --> 00:38:21,245
So a few of the guys from down south actually moved up north during the last six months of development and started making Diablo into multiplayer and integrating Battle.net into the entire thing.

428
00:38:21,285 --> 00:38:24,906
And so I didn't know anything about multiplayer.

429
00:38:24,926 --> 00:38:26,786
I didn't know anything about networking at the time.

430
00:38:27,386 --> 00:38:29,487
I learned a lot through the process of doing this.

431
00:38:29,547 --> 00:38:32,568
And actually, we were able to do all the networking stuff for Diablo 2.

432
00:38:34,108 --> 00:38:34,288
that

433
00:38:53,549 --> 00:38:58,853
we didn't realize, like it just didn't dawn on us, like we knew people were going to be able to hack because it was peer-to-peer.

434
00:38:59,594 --> 00:39:11,743
And that means that everybody's kind of in charge of their own shit and we've got, you know, whatever, we're going to do our own stuff right here and, you know, if you want to cheat, go ahead and ruin your little game for yourself, that's fine, we don't really care.

435
00:39:13,564 --> 00:39:17,968
And then it came out and like instantly we're like, oh my God,

436
00:39:19,125 --> 00:39:22,307
they can just upload the cheats and everybody can cheat.

437
00:39:23,468 --> 00:39:25,289
Oh my God, I didn't even think about that.

438
00:39:25,389 --> 00:39:32,575
So obviously the number one thing that we wanted to fix with Diablo was the rampant cheating that happened.

439
00:39:32,595 --> 00:39:36,778
And so going client server for Diablo 2 was definitely necessary.

440
00:39:36,818 --> 00:39:39,720
So some of the good things and some of the bad things there.

441
00:39:43,327 --> 00:39:45,549
DirectX and PC Gamer CD.

442
00:39:46,549 --> 00:40:05,803
So late in the summertime, in the kind of September-ish time frame, DirectX was kind of taking off, and Windows 95 was kind of taking off, and DOS really sucked, because it was really hard to run games under DOS, because you had to know, hey, what's your IRQ of your sound card?

443
00:40:05,843 --> 00:40:11,387
And is this extended memory, or expanded memory, or blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, and you had to have, you know,

444
00:40:12,568 --> 00:40:16,813
PhD in computer science to actually run games.

445
00:40:17,934 --> 00:40:20,356
DirectX was going to solve all those problems.

446
00:40:20,997 --> 00:40:29,646
Now that's what they had said with WinG as well, so people were a little reluctant, which they dropped like a hot potato, and so I don't know about this DirectX thing.

447
00:40:29,687 --> 00:40:33,050
So we kind of waited a little while, and then DirectX said, well,

448
00:40:33,986 --> 00:40:40,009
We would like there to be a demo of Diablo on this CD, and we're going to send out a million copies of the CD.

449
00:40:40,029 --> 00:40:43,951
And we said, OK, I think we'll make DirectX now.

450
00:40:45,372 --> 00:40:59,260
And so we made the DirectX version, and it got distributed, as well as we used the demo and sent it to PC Gamer, and they distributed it in their November 1996 magazine, which came out in October.

451
00:41:01,075 --> 00:41:12,800
and uh... and so i was kind of rummaging downstairs and actually found the disk and i actually got it running and here it is this is a little bit of the demo right here

452
00:41:30,355 --> 00:41:32,316
Now you'll notice a few things here.

453
00:41:32,336 --> 00:41:37,017
The interface is much different.

454
00:41:37,097 --> 00:41:43,399
The panels are reversed.

455
00:41:43,419 --> 00:41:45,919
The speech for this guy is very different.

456
00:41:45,959 --> 00:41:48,380
I barely escaped from the butcher.

457
00:41:48,840 --> 00:41:51,741
He killed my wife, my children.

458
00:41:52,461 --> 00:41:55,242
I beg of you, in the name of God,

459
00:41:56,071 --> 00:41:57,611
Avenge them.

460
00:41:58,112 --> 00:42:00,552
I believe that was Bill Roper, but I'm not positive.

461
00:42:02,833 --> 00:42:04,533
The smell of death surrounds me.

462
00:42:08,154 --> 00:42:10,734
Notice how I miss all sorts of stuff.

463
00:42:12,335 --> 00:42:13,715
How am I missing that barrel?

464
00:42:17,216 --> 00:42:19,837
One other thing is that you had to click to move.

465
00:42:19,877 --> 00:42:20,977
You couldn't click and hold.

466
00:42:21,277 --> 00:42:24,258
It was like clickety, clickety, clickety, clickety, clickety all the time.

467
00:42:24,298 --> 00:42:25,438
There was no click and hold.

468
00:42:29,020 --> 00:42:32,363
And our favorite.

469
00:42:32,883 --> 00:42:33,664
Fresh meat.

470
00:42:33,844 --> 00:42:34,305
Oh yeah.

471
00:42:34,625 --> 00:42:41,030
The butcher was on level one.

472
00:42:53,240 --> 00:42:54,441
I can't believe that still ran.

473
00:42:54,461 --> 00:42:56,342
That was amazing.

474
00:42:56,382 --> 00:42:58,024
I was so excited when I got that thing running.

475
00:42:59,893 --> 00:43:18,212
So one of the things with that CD that really we got tons of great feedback and out of that came a strike team, which I'll talk about in a second, that kind of processed that feedback and we used that feedback to kind of iterate on the game and the interface and things like that.

476
00:43:19,073 --> 00:43:19,433
talk about

477
00:43:34,660 --> 00:43:44,187
25 minutes of character creation it was Tell me about your backstory and like where what town do you come from and what color are your eyes?

478
00:43:44,268 --> 00:43:52,134
And like all the which is great and stuff But it was like it was 20 minutes to actually get in when I loved about NHL 95 was like clickety-click quick I'm skating.

479
00:43:52,154 --> 00:43:53,415
I'm skating around and body checking.

480
00:43:53,435 --> 00:43:56,898
I'm like and so it was the it was like that quick quick action

481
00:43:56,918 --> 00:43:57,198
session.

482
00:44:12,827 --> 00:44:18,009
which is kind of terrible to say at this point, but it was back then and it applied a little bit more.

483
00:44:18,029 --> 00:44:21,570
My mom actually became a computer expert, but at the time she was not.

484
00:44:21,630 --> 00:44:27,052
And so it was, would my mom be able to play this, was really kind of the test.

485
00:44:27,812 --> 00:44:30,754
And sure enough, she could, and she liked it.

486
00:44:30,794 --> 00:44:32,014
Actually, she became pretty good at it.

487
00:44:33,094 --> 00:44:37,696
My father-in-law actually still plays a ton of Diablo II, but that's beside the point.

488
00:44:38,957 --> 00:44:39,437
So anyway,

489
00:44:41,548 --> 00:44:46,531
we really want to create the simplest interface possible and I thought that it was very, very effective.

490
00:44:47,631 --> 00:44:53,374
One debate that we had, that was a very hotly debated thing, was what to do with the automap.

491
00:44:54,235 --> 00:45:05,721
At the time, a lot of the automaps in role-playing games were like a piece of parchment and it drew the grid and you could put your pen on it and you could type in notes and you could do all these kind of things.

492
00:45:05,741 --> 00:45:08,943
It was the wrong pace for the game, completely.

493
00:45:09,523 --> 00:45:12,884
At the time, a game called Dark Forces came out, which is one of my favorite games.

494
00:45:13,544 --> 00:45:14,945
And I love that game.

495
00:45:15,665 --> 00:45:21,246
And they had this cool overlay on top of the screen.

496
00:45:21,866 --> 00:45:26,848
So it became this kind of almost meme around the office in which

497
00:45:28,488 --> 00:45:32,312
There was a lot of controversy, and finally, are we going to do something modern and cool?

498
00:45:32,572 --> 00:45:37,996
Are we going to do the cool dark forces way, or are we going to do this crappy old parchment way?

499
00:45:38,517 --> 00:45:48,005
And eventually, a lot of things, it was this, all right, we're going to cool dark forces, this was the code for we're going to do kind of the modern thing.

500
00:45:48,025 --> 00:45:50,987
So that led to the automap, which changed a lot.

501
00:45:51,748 --> 00:45:56,009
The strike team gave us a bunch of feedback on the interface and we did a whole bunch of changes.

502
00:45:56,270 --> 00:46:06,413
One thing that you will notice that I was shocked to remember is that we invented the hot bar sometime in the last three months of the project.

503
00:46:08,254 --> 00:46:19,998
On the left, on the left screen in the very lower left, there was one slot where you could put one potion down in the kind of like gold background on the lower left.

504
00:46:23,130 --> 00:46:24,952
The interface got much nicer.

505
00:46:25,012 --> 00:46:25,973
We cleaned it up.

506
00:46:25,993 --> 00:46:32,279
I think that, in all fairness, the interface got much nicer simply because we kept changing it over and over and over again.

507
00:46:32,299 --> 00:46:35,642
So we didn't want to make it look too nice until it was the final version.

508
00:46:36,843 --> 00:46:41,048
And it was laid out much nicer, and the experience became much better.

509
00:46:41,068 --> 00:46:46,373
But a lot of this feedback was from the PC Gamer and DirectX demo.

510
00:46:47,761 --> 00:46:53,926
The strike team also gave us some very strange suggestions that I'd like to point out at this time.

511
00:46:54,306 --> 00:46:57,128
One was that they wanted to remove the right mouse button.

512
00:46:58,029 --> 00:47:00,671
They didn't want right mouse button to be using skills.

513
00:47:00,811 --> 00:47:09,137
They wanted you to have to bring the skill book up, click on the skill, and then click on the monster or whatever in the window.

514
00:47:10,823 --> 00:47:13,764
That was very controversial for weeks and weeks.

515
00:47:14,385 --> 00:47:19,327
But the most controversial is they wanted cooking and food to be in the game.

516
00:47:21,708 --> 00:47:23,569
I can't even imagine what that would have been like.

517
00:47:23,909 --> 00:47:27,911
Well, I kind of sort of can, because they put food in World of Warcraft.

518
00:47:27,931 --> 00:47:36,775
So I think that the premise was the same, but Diablo with a food meter was definitely not on the list.

519
00:47:39,157 --> 00:47:39,877
What went wrong?

520
00:47:42,263 --> 00:47:44,967
So here's a little bit of a personal story.

521
00:47:46,109 --> 00:47:47,552
The end was really, really rough.

522
00:47:47,732 --> 00:47:54,222
We crunched probably for the last eight or nine months, something like that.

523
00:47:55,745 --> 00:47:56,486
And it was,

524
00:47:58,507 --> 00:47:59,868
It was all day, every day.

525
00:48:00,249 --> 00:48:04,832
I would get up, usually at about four in the morning, and I would drive to work.

526
00:48:04,872 --> 00:48:07,294
I'd pick up my brother, and we'd go to work.

527
00:48:07,454 --> 00:48:13,438
And then I would work until, I don't know, midnight or so, and go home, and get up and do it again.

528
00:48:14,279 --> 00:48:17,882
And I did this for every day for about eight months.

529
00:48:20,604 --> 00:48:23,506
My wife was pregnant at the time.

530
00:48:25,347 --> 00:48:27,189
The baby was due in late December.

531
00:48:29,264 --> 00:48:30,485
you can see where this is going.

532
00:48:34,046 --> 00:48:37,748
Around December 10th, my wife calls.

533
00:48:37,788 --> 00:48:38,529
I'm at the office.

534
00:48:40,089 --> 00:48:43,311
And she says, I'm having contractions.

535
00:48:44,432 --> 00:48:46,673
It's go time.

536
00:48:46,933 --> 00:48:47,753
Wait, what?

537
00:48:48,574 --> 00:48:49,614
But I'm in the middle of this.

538
00:48:49,994 --> 00:48:50,695
Oh, my God.

539
00:48:53,678 --> 00:48:56,561
And so we were already burning things, you know, left and right.

540
00:48:56,581 --> 00:48:58,222
We really tried to make Christmas.

541
00:48:58,502 --> 00:49:01,805
We desperately wanted to make Christmas, but it just wasn't ready.

542
00:49:02,586 --> 00:49:05,469
And here was, you know, kind of my worst case scenario.

543
00:49:07,331 --> 00:49:11,715
Fortunately, in some ways, it turned out to be a false alarm.

544
00:49:12,055 --> 00:49:15,338
And the baby didn't, wasn't born then, actually was born

545
00:49:16,597 --> 00:49:17,238
on January 3rd.

546
00:49:30,502 --> 00:49:35,045
One of the things about Crunch, I think Crunch gets a little bit of a bad rap, and it should.

547
00:49:36,186 --> 00:49:40,988
But for me, personally, it is something that I thrive under.

548
00:49:41,549 --> 00:49:55,057
And I know that it's not really talked about much as a positive thing, but I think Crunch can be a positive thing if it's handled correctly and if your partner is tolerant enough of such a schedule.

549
00:49:57,018 --> 00:50:02,702
But I think that for me, we really came together as a team.

550
00:50:02,722 --> 00:50:04,183
We were always very, very close.

551
00:50:04,203 --> 00:50:05,623
We would do all sorts of things together.

552
00:50:05,643 --> 00:50:10,867
We hung out at the office all the time, and went out to lunch every day, and did all sorts of things together.

553
00:50:12,528 --> 00:50:18,872
But really, those kind of stories, and the late night hours, and the goofiness that happens, as well as like, you know,

554
00:50:19,532 --> 00:50:29,264
putting in cows that you can click on and things like that, they end up happening late at night in kind of the stress and goofiness of the entire situation.

555
00:50:30,065 --> 00:50:36,012
So it was really rough and it was difficult personally and I was quite burnt out for about two or three months afterwards.

556
00:50:39,282 --> 00:50:41,823
I thought that it really made the product shine.

557
00:50:41,843 --> 00:50:45,044
We were super ultra focused on making this thing great.

558
00:50:45,784 --> 00:50:48,965
And that really made a big difference in the end.

559
00:50:49,425 --> 00:50:55,967
So though crunch is never fun, I think that sometimes, especially for myself, it's a necessary evil.

560
00:50:57,768 --> 00:50:59,928
So, kind of the home stretch.

561
00:51:00,268 --> 00:51:03,169
We finally got everything in the game, including Diablo.

562
00:51:03,189 --> 00:51:05,770
Diablo got put in, and so we decided we were going to

563
00:51:07,187 --> 00:51:08,507
put the whole game to a test.

564
00:51:08,687 --> 00:51:11,428
We need people to kind of play and iterate and work on this.

565
00:51:11,988 --> 00:51:15,369
So let's offer up a $100 prize for the first person.

566
00:51:15,589 --> 00:51:18,630
And we're only paying people $12,000 a year.

567
00:51:19,390 --> 00:51:21,110
That's quite a $100 prize.

568
00:51:21,130 --> 00:51:21,770
It means something.

569
00:51:21,831 --> 00:51:25,211
So let's go ahead and pay $100 for the first person that kills Diablo.

570
00:51:32,634 --> 00:51:44,083
and so we had this skill in the game called blood exchange at the time and so what this skill did is it swapped your hit points with a monster

571
00:51:48,213 --> 00:51:50,174
Well, we didn't think that through, did we?

572
00:51:51,214 --> 00:51:54,936
And so this person, Ben Haas, he ended up finding the skill.

573
00:51:55,196 --> 00:51:57,037
Again, he runs down to Diablo.

574
00:51:57,317 --> 00:51:58,237
He swaps his health.

575
00:51:58,257 --> 00:51:59,078
He's like nearly dead.

576
00:51:59,278 --> 00:52:01,239
He swaps his health, and he kills Diablo and went below.

577
00:52:01,799 --> 00:52:04,700
And we promptly yanked the skill out of the game.

578
00:52:08,101 --> 00:52:09,702
So anyway, that was it.

579
00:52:09,802 --> 00:52:15,044
And then Diablo finally got finished and was released on December 31st.

580
00:52:17,187 --> 00:52:26,611
1996 and turned out to be something that I'm very proud of and something that will be remembered for a long time.

581
00:52:26,691 --> 00:52:30,493
And I'm just so happy that I was able to participate in it and so lucky.

582
00:52:31,873 --> 00:52:32,693
Thank you, everybody.

583
00:52:33,314 --> 00:52:36,135
And I will take questions if people have some.

584
00:52:36,215 --> 00:52:37,055
So go ahead.

585
00:52:53,783 --> 00:52:54,724
Yes, go ahead.

586
00:52:54,784 --> 00:52:55,044
Hi there.

587
00:52:55,304 --> 00:52:56,045
Thanks for this talk.

588
00:52:56,085 --> 00:52:56,645
It was really cool.

589
00:52:56,686 --> 00:52:58,067
I'm a huge fan of the original game.

590
00:52:58,747 --> 00:53:01,189
I think it's a really foundational game.

591
00:53:01,229 --> 00:53:02,410
I think it's a really important game.

592
00:53:03,411 --> 00:53:07,475
Isn't it true that there's no way to buy and play Diablo now?

593
00:53:09,356 --> 00:53:10,337
I do not know.

594
00:53:10,357 --> 00:53:13,060
I have a copy of Diablo right here.

595
00:53:13,520 --> 00:53:15,522
OK, well, the Amazon Marketplace is certainly one.

596
00:53:15,622 --> 00:53:16,463
It's unopened.

597
00:53:17,223 --> 00:53:17,584
Oh, wow.

598
00:53:17,684 --> 00:53:19,605
OK, you should put it on the Amazon Marketplace.

599
00:53:20,746 --> 00:53:21,187
Do you want it?

600
00:53:22,465 --> 00:53:25,526
I have one.

601
00:53:25,546 --> 00:53:27,166
Thank you.

602
00:53:27,226 --> 00:53:29,526
Preservation of this stuff seems really important.

603
00:53:29,907 --> 00:53:30,407
I agree.

604
00:53:30,847 --> 00:53:32,527
I would like to see it still working.

605
00:53:32,547 --> 00:53:33,507
I don't know if it does.

606
00:53:33,527 --> 00:53:35,588
I mean, I got it to work, but I have an old laptop.

607
00:53:35,628 --> 00:53:39,629
It runs on XP still, but XP is going by the wayside.

608
00:53:39,649 --> 00:53:44,750
I didn't try installing it on Windows 10 or anything like that, but I know that it did work on XP.

609
00:53:47,831 --> 00:53:59,080
First off, thank you for the talk.

610
00:53:59,160 --> 00:54:02,663
It's great for fanboys like me who got into the industry because of this game.

611
00:54:03,724 --> 00:54:04,805
I have a two-part question.

612
00:54:05,085 --> 00:54:09,689
The first one is, how did you guys come up with Deckard Cain and the line, stay a while and listen?

613
00:54:10,910 --> 00:54:12,351
I don't remember exactly how we got it.

614
00:54:12,591 --> 00:54:14,233
However, Deckard Cain's name

615
00:54:15,723 --> 00:54:17,484
was actually a contest.

616
00:54:18,924 --> 00:54:34,750
So as part of a gaming PC magazine, I think it was Computer Gaming World or something like that, we had this contest where people could send in name suggestions, or maybe it was their names, to get their name in the video game Diablo.

617
00:54:35,370 --> 00:54:38,132
And some guy sent in the name Deckard Cain.

618
00:54:38,172 --> 00:54:40,813
I don't know if it's made up or not, but we're like, damn, that's badass.

619
00:54:40,853 --> 00:54:41,933
We're going to use that.

620
00:54:42,333 --> 00:54:44,696
And so that's how the name came about, actually.

621
00:54:44,776 --> 00:54:45,657
It was this person.

622
00:54:46,037 --> 00:54:49,661
Somewhere out there, there is an actual Decker Cain walking around, I think.

623
00:54:50,702 --> 00:54:52,904
And he's like, I'm famous.

624
00:54:53,404 --> 00:54:54,786
Stay a while and listen to my story.

625
00:54:56,227 --> 00:54:58,569
And so I don't know where the line came from.

626
00:54:59,831 --> 00:55:03,855
I didn't actually write the dialogue, but that's how the name came about.

627
00:55:04,515 --> 00:55:05,136
Thank you.

628
00:55:09,072 --> 00:55:12,536
So you talked about a few of the influences for Diablo during the talk.

629
00:55:12,596 --> 00:55:14,978
I was just wondering if there were any more that you could bring up?

630
00:55:15,398 --> 00:55:16,179
I'd be interested to hear.

631
00:55:16,239 --> 00:55:17,561
Oh my god, it's a giant list.

632
00:55:17,741 --> 00:55:19,182
I'm sure, yeah.

633
00:55:19,683 --> 00:55:25,508
Yeah, I mean, Ultima, Wizardry, many different roguelikes.

634
00:55:26,209 --> 00:55:27,550
Doom was an influence on us.

635
00:55:27,570 --> 00:55:29,232
Like I said, everything from

636
00:55:30,393 --> 00:55:30,493
at

637
00:55:47,643 --> 00:55:55,665
Yes, well definitely in the art style there was quite a bit of influence from gothic art style and things like that.

638
00:55:55,705 --> 00:56:07,269
There was a church that one of the artists had visited on some vacation that he used actually to model the Tristram church and things like that.

639
00:56:07,409 --> 00:56:12,290
Those influences were in real life as well as kind of European old style architecture.

640
00:56:12,811 --> 00:56:13,451
Cool, thanks very much.

641
00:56:17,048 --> 00:56:22,933
I sat down one Friday evening and got up that Sunday evening having tied up the phone lines for an entire three days.

642
00:56:37,395 --> 00:56:50,249
